        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the NYC market",
        "market_text": "<p>New York buyers make snap judgments on StreetEasy, and the city's housing stock makes empty photos brutal: pre-war co-ops with narrow rooms, new-development glass boxes that all look alike, and railroad layouts buyers can't mentally furnish. Home staging proves a queen bed fits in the second bedroom — the question that decides whether an NYC showing gets booked.</p><p>Physical staging in Manhattan and Brooklyn is a serious expense: furniture rental, a service elevator booking, a certificate of insurance for the building, and a crew who can work within board rules. Virtual home staging skips all of it, which is why the cost comparison here is more lopsided than in any other US market.</p><p>Co-op and condo board logistics are the hidden cost people forget. A physical stage can stall for weeks waiting on building approval and elevator access; the photos can't wait that long when a listing's first week on StreetEasy determines most of its traffic.</p>",
